1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:11,100
Hello everyone! I'm NTC, the assistant of Ms. Sorn Leakena from the Governance, Risk & Compliance team — GRC.

2
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:19,370
We are a cloud and internet service provider, so our customers' data is our most valuable asset.

3
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:24,460
Today I'll take you through information security awareness.

4
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:30,560
This is a refresher on information security awareness.

5
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:38,300
Some of you are here because you were flagged in our recent phishing simulation — and that's completely fine.

6
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:46,680
The goal isn't to call anyone out; it's to make sure we all recognize the same red flags before a real attacker tests us.

7
00:00:48,130 --> 00:01:06,420
Here's what we'll cover: why this matters, best practices across four everyday risk areas, how we classify information, social engineering and phishing, email and Telegram security, and the ISO standards that support it all.

8
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:19,260
So what is this? It's teaching everyone — not just IT — how to recognize threats, protect sensitive data and build safe digital habits.

9
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:27,720
Security isn't only a technical problem; it's a people problem, and people are what today is about.

10
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:37,020
Four reasons this matters. One — it reduces human error, still the number one cause of breaches.

11
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:41,940
Two — it protects our data and our reputation with clients.

12
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:47,020
Three — it builds a culture where security is everyone's job.

13
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:50,940
Four — it helps us meet ISO 27001 and ISO 9001.

14
00:01:52,390 --> 00:02:02,290
We'll walk through four common weak spots: password security, internet safety, clear desk and clear screen, and using your own devices.

15
00:02:02,290 --> 00:02:07,200
These are the basics — but basics are exactly where most incidents start.

16
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:16,330
Simple rules: never share your password, change it regularly, and turn on 2FA wherever you can.

17
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:31,180
Use at least 12 characters, mix upper and lower case, numbers and symbols, use a different password for every account, and never use anything guessable like birthdays or pet names.

18
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:40,910
This chart shows why it matters. A weak password like 123456 can be cracked in seconds.

19
00:02:40,910 --> 00:02:48,070
A good one takes months to years. A strong, random one can take centuries.

20
00:02:48,070 --> 00:02:53,400
The difference is just a little effort, once, at setup.

21
00:02:54,700 --> 00:03:03,020
HTTP versus HTTPS: without encryption, anything you type — even passwords — can be intercepted.

22
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:09,150
Always check for the padlock and https before entering sensitive data.

23
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:14,310
Download only from trusted sources and avoid untrusted ads.

24
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:21,580
Remember: you have to be right every time; an attacker only needs to be right once.

25
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:29,320
Five habits: lock your screen with Windows + L every time you step away.

26
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:33,610
Keep sensitive files and USB drives locked away.

27
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:39,240
Wipe whiteboards after meetings. Pick up printouts immediately.

28
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:43,000
And never write passwords on sticky notes.

29
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:51,390
Using a personal device for work? Install the required firewall and antivirus.

30
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:57,480
Keep the system and apps updated. MFA is mandatory — no exceptions.

31
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:06,940
Encrypt data at rest and in transit. If a device is lost or stolen, contact the IT Helpdesk immediately.

32
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:12,170
We classify information into four tiers:

33
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:20,910
Public, Internal, Confidential and Restricted — sensitivity increases from left to right.

34
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:28,760
The tier tells you how carefully it must be handled and who's allowed to see it.

35
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:45,020
Phishing is a scam sent by email, SMS or phone call, designed to steal credentials or install malware — usually by getting you to click a link and enter information, or download an attachment.

36
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:54,200
Five checks before trusting any email: verify the sender's real address, not just the name.

37
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,490
Watch for urgency, threats or rewards.

38
00:04:57,490 --> 00:05:01,300
Don't click suspicious links or attachments.

39
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:09,440
Look for odd formatting or grammar. About 80% of unexpected 'urgent' emails could be phishing.

40
00:05:10,890 --> 00:05:21,840
Never open files ending in .exe, .bat, .vbs, .html or .scr from an untrusted source — they are almost always malware.

41
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:30,700
Word, Excel, PowerPoint, JPEG and PDF are lower risk, but still verify the sender first.

42
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:40,700
Suspicious email? Do not click, and do not forward it to colleagues — that only spreads the risk.

43
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:46,710
Report it with the official reporting tool, which alerts IT and blocks the sender.

44
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:48,560
Then delete it.

45
00:05:49,860 --> 00:06:05,670
Since a lot of internal communication happens on Telegram, check four settings today: turn on 2FA, end suspicious sessions, turn off automatic file download, and turn on a local passcode.

46
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:12,100
All four are in Settings → Privacy and Security, and take about two minutes.

47
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:29,280
ISO 27001 is the standard for information security management — often required by clients, it reduces our risk of breaches, from leadership commitment to continual improvement.

48
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:39,120
ISO 9001 is our quality standard: stronger client trust, fewer errors and an edge when bidding for contracts.

49
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:45,270
Bringing it together: strong, unique passwords with 2FA.

50
00:06:45,270 --> 00:06:49,940
HTTPS-only browsing. A clear desk and a locked screen.

51
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:55,740
Updated, encrypted personal devices. Know your data classification.

52
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:59,380
Pause before clicking anything suspicious.

53
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:02,060
Secure your email and Telegram.

54
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:10,830
Thank you all for your attention. Remember: information security is everyone's responsibility.

55
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:18,150
If you have a question or see something suspicious, contact the GRC team or the IT Helpdesk.

56
00:07:18,150 --> 00:07:22,280
This is NTC, Ms. Sorn Leakena's assistant — goodbye!
